### Estimands



### Identification



### Definitions {.unnumbered}





::: {#def-graph .theorem}
# Graph

:::





::: {#def-bipartite-graph .theorem}
# Bipartite graph

:::



::: {#def-exposure-mapping .theorem}
# Exposure mapping

:::



::: {#def-separability .theorem}
# Separability

We say exposure mapping $g_i$ is separable if

$$
g_i(\mathbf{X}_{1}, ..., \mathbf{X}_{K}) = [g_0,    g_{1i}(\mathbf{X}_{1}), ...,   g_{Ki}(\mathbf{X}_{K})]^T
$$

where $g_0 = [X_{1i}, ..., X_{Ki}]^T$ and $g_{ti}() \in \mathbf{R}$. We call the functions $g_{ti}$ component functions of exposure mapping $g_i$.

:::


::: {#def-loyalty .theorem}
# Loyalty

We say a component function $g_{ti}$ is loyal to graph $\mathscr{G}$ if for $\mathbf{X},\mathbf{X}' \in \{0,1\}^n$





$$
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{X}_{t\mathscr{N}_i} = \mathbf{X'}_{t\mathscr{N}_i}
\implies 
g_{ti}(\mathbf{X}) = g_{ti}( \mathbf{X'}) 
\end{equation}
$$
:::


::: {#def-positional-invariance .theorem}
# Positional invariance

We say component functions $g_{ti}$ are positionally invariant if for $i,j \in \mathscr{N}$, $\mathbf{X},\mathbf{X}' \in \{0,1\}^n$ and conformable permutation matrix $\mathbf{M}$:

$$
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{X}_{\mathscr{N}_i} = \mathbf{M} \mathbf{X'}_{\mathscr{N}_j} \text{ and } \Psi_{i\mathscr{N}_i} =  \mathbf{M} \Psi_{j\mathscr{N}_j}\\
\implies \\
g_{ti}(\mathbf{X}) = g_{tj}(\mathbf{X'}) 
\end{equation}
$$
:::



::: {#def-boundedness .theorem}
# Boundedness

We say that a network exposure mapping $g_{t}$ is bounded if for all $\mathbf{X} \in \{0,1\}^n, \mathbf{W} \in \mathbf{R}^n_+$ all possible network exposure values are contained by a finite set $\mathbf{D}$. i.e.: 

:::: {.aside}

See note on boundedness assumption, below.

::::

$$
g_t(\mathbf{X}, \mathbf{W})  \in \mathbf{D} 
$$

with $|\mathbf{D}| < c \in \mathbf{R}$ where $c$ is not a function of the number of interconnected units in graph $\mathscr{G}$.



:::




::: {#def-homogenous-interference .theorem}
# Homogenous network interference

:::


::: {#def-nffrcistg .theorem}
# Network FFRCISTG

A NFFRCISTG is a FFRCISTG associated with a graph. Each node in the graph represents an observational unit in the FFRCISTG. A network tie indicates a pair of observational units whose measures are causes of each other. All the structural equations in this model have exposure mappings which are separable with well-defined and bounded network exposure mappings and independent errors.

:::






## Social and spatial inference





### Example 3 {.unnumbered}



### Intro to DANGs and SWINGs {.unnumbered}




```{r  include=FALSE}
targets::tar_source("../R_functions")
```

```{r}
#| fig-height: 8
#| fig-width: 5
#| label: fig-causal-dang-instructions
#| fig-cap: Causal DANG

structural <- example_dang_1_structural() |>
                    plot_swig(node_radius = 0.2) +
                    scale_fill_mpxnyc(option = "light") +
                    theme_mpxnyc_blank()+
                    ggplot2::ggtitle("Structural Part") 

relational <- example_dang_1_relational() |>
                    plot_swig(0, 0, node_radius = 0.2)  +
                    scale_fill_mpxnyc(name = "Entity type", option = "light") +
                    theme_mpxnyc_blank() +
                    ggplot2::ggtitle("Relational Part") 

cowplot::plot_grid(structural, relational, ncol = 1)

```

```{r  include=FALSE}
targets::tar_source("../R_functions")
```

```{r}
#| fig-height: 6
#| fig-width: 8
#| fig-align: center
#| label: fig-example-dang-structural
#| fig-cap: "Causal DANG for Week 1 in Example 3"


structural  <- example_dang_2_structural() |>
                    plot_swig(node_radius = 0.15) +
                    scale_fill_mpxnyc(option = "light") +
                    theme_mpxnyc_blank() +
                    ggplot2::ggtitle("Structural Part")


relational  <- example_dang_2_relational() |>
                    plot_swig(node_radius = 0.2, node_margin = 0)  +
                    scale_fill_mpxnyc(name = "Entity type", option = "light") +
                    theme_mpxnyc_blank() +
                    ggplot2::ggtitle("Relational Part") 

figure   <- cowplot::plot_grid(structural, relational, ncol=1, rel_heights = c(3,3))



legend_pre  <- relational + ggplot2::theme(legend.position = "right")

legend      <- cowplot::get_legend(legend_pre) 
 
   
cowplot::plot_grid(figure, legend, nrow = 1, rel_widths = c(4,1))
```

